detector_raw:
{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "What is the objective of this packing plan — are we minimizing the number of containers used, maximizing the total weight (or total units) of goods shipped, or something else?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy choice regarding the optimization objective, presenting mutually exclusive options for one decision."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "You said we want to pack all the goods — does that mean every available unit of every type (all 120 A, 90 B, 300 C, 90 D, 120 E) must be loaded, or is it acceptable to leave some units unshipped if that lets us use fewer containers?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy choice: whether all units must be loaded or if some can be left unshipped. This is one semantically independent question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Since we're minimizing the number of containers and not required to ship everything, is it acceptable for a container to hold zero units of some type (e.g., no A, no B, no E), as long as it meets the 18–60 ton weight range and the 12-unit D minimum?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response contains a single clarification question asking whether containers can have zero units of certain types while meeting other constraints."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Are the unit counts for each goods type in each container required to be whole numbers (i.e., you can't split a unit of goods across containers)?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response contains a single clarification question asking whether unit counts must be integers."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "When you say \"at least 12 units of type D goods\" per container, does this minimum apply to every container that is used, or only to containers that actually contain some type D goods?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response contains a single clarification question asking for a specific policy choice regarding the scope of a minimum constraint."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "The rule says a container with any type A must also contain at least one type C — is this a one-way requirement only (A implies C), or does it also work the other way (a container with C must also contain A)?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy clarification regarding the directionality of the constraint between type A and type C."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "The 18-ton minimum applies to \"any container that is used\" — does this mean every container we count in our plan must be loaded to at least 18 tons, or could we have partially-filled containers below 18 tons that we simply don't count toward the total?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy clarification regarding the scope of the 18-ton minimum rule, presenting two mutually exclusive interpretations of the same constraint."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Is there a maximum number of containers available, or is the number of containers we can use unlimited (we just want to minimize it)?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy choice regarding container availability limits, which constitutes one independent clarification question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "should I proceed with the assumption that each unit of goods is indivisible (i.e., you can only load whole units of each type into each container)?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single confirmation on whether to assume indivisibility of units."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Since we're minimizing containers and not required to ship all goods, is there any reason we'd want to ship more than the minimum necessary — for example, is there a secondary goal to maximize the total weight shipped among all solutions that use the minimum number of containers?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question about whether a secondary objective (maximizing weight) exists within the primary constraint (minimizing containers)."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Is there any constraint on the total number of units of a single goods type that can go into one container (e.g., a container cannot hold more than X units of type C), or is the only per-container limit the 60-ton weight cap and the 12-unit D minimum?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding whether there are per-type unit limits in addition to the stated weight and minimum unit constraints."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Are there any other constraints or business rules I should be aware of that aren't covered by the 18–60 ton weight range, the 12-unit D minimum, the A-implies-C rule, the per-type availability caps, and the indivisibility of units?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response contains a single clarification question asking if there are any additional constraints or rules not already listed."
}
